<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="{{@site.locale}}">
<head>

    {{!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         HEAD ORDER IS A PERFORMANCE DECISION, NOT A STYLE ONE.

         The browser parses this top to bottom and cannot start a request it
         has not read yet, so the order below is roughly "cheapest and most
         blocking first". Moving a line up or down here changes LCP.

           1  charset    must land inside the first 1024 bytes or the parser
                         restarts having guessed wrong
           2  viewport   layout depends on it; it is ~50 bytes
           3  title      what a share preview and a tab need first
           4  preconnect open the sockets we KNOW will be used, while the
                         parser is still reading
           5  preload    the two fonts that draw above the fold
           6  theme-init INLINE and blocking, before the stylesheet
           7  stylesheet the render-blocking one
           8  ghost_head everything Ghost derives from the content
           9  JSON-LD    ours, after Ghost's
          10  services   analytics, and last on purpose
         ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ --}}

    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>{{meta_title}}</title>

    {{!-- ── 4 · Sockets we actually use ──────────────────────────────────
         ⚠ ONLY ORIGINS THIS PAGE GENUINELY FETCHES FROM. jsdelivr serves
         Ghost's Portal and search bundles, which are injected by
         {{ghost_head}} further down — the preconnect buys back the DNS +
         TLS round trip that would otherwise start only once the parser
         reached that script.

         Everything else the page links to — GitHub, X, the social row — is
         an ANCHOR, not a resource. Preconnecting to those opens sockets that
         are never used, which costs the very round trips this is meant to
         save. A preconnect list is a claim about what loads; keep it true.

         Analytics origins are NOT here: each service partial declares its own
         only when its key is set, so a site with no analytics opens no
         sockets for it. --}}
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net" crossorigin>

    {{!-- ── 5 · The two faces that draw above the fold ────────────────────
         The @font-face rules live inside the compiled stylesheet, so the
         browser only discovers them after fetching and parsing it — one
         round trip later than it could start. Preloading closes that gap,
         which is what keeps the fallback swap invisible on a cold load.

         Only these two. A preload for something not used immediately is a
         console warning and bandwidth taken from something that was.

         `crossorigin` is REQUIRED even though these are same-origin — fonts
         are always fetched in CORS mode, and a preload without it produces a
         second, unused download instead of a warm cache hit. It is the most
         commonly broken line in any theme's head. --}}
    <link rel="preload" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin
          href="{{asset "fonts/switzer-var-latin.woff2"}}">
    <link rel="preload" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin
          href="{{asset "fonts/roboto-mono-var-latin.woff2"}}">

    {{!-- ── 6 · Theme, before the first paint ─────────────────────────────
         Inline and blocking, and it must stay both. A <script src> is
         fetched separately and lands after the page has already painted,
         which is the white-flash-on-every-navigation bug this exists to
         prevent. It sets data-theme on <html>, which the stylesheet below
         then reads — so it has to run first. --}}
    {{> "theme-init"}}

    {{!-- ── 7 · The stylesheet ────────────────────────────────────────────
         One file, always named .min.css. See gulpfile.mjs for why the name
         is constant while the contents differ between dev and production. --}}
    <link rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="{{asset "built/screen.min.css"}}">

    {{!-- ── 8 · Ghost's own head ──────────────────────────────────────────
         Open Graph, Twitter cards, canonical URLs, the publisher's accent
         colour, Article/WebSite JSON-LD, Members, search, and the RSS
         alternate. NEVER hand-write any of it: a theme that emits its own
         og:title ends up with two, and Ghost's is the one that follows what
         the editor set. --}}
    {{ghost_head}}

    {{!-- ── 9 · What Ghost cannot know ────────────────────────────────────
         The main menu as SiteNavigationElement, from the same {{navigation}}
         helper the visible bar uses — one source, so the markup and the
         structured data cannot describe different menus. A second block is
         valid and is how a theme ADDS to what the platform describes rather
         than replacing it. abstract/06-discoverability.md. --}}
    {{navigation variant="jsonld"}}

</head>
<body class="{{body_class}}">

    {{!-- FIRST element inside <body>, per NSDS's skip-link archetype. The
         target below needs id="main" AND tabindex="-1" — without the
         tabindex, Safari and Chrome move the viewport but leave focus
         behind, and the link accomplishes nothing. --}}
    <a class="ns-skip-link ns-visually-hidden" href="#main">{{t "Skip to content"}}</a>

    {{!-- ── CHROME DISPATCH ───────────────────────────────────────────────
      A reading surface must not get the marketing bar. Reading kinds carry
      their own chrome inside their own partial — the training rail, the
      course player — so this only decides whether the SITE bar appears.

      ⚠ SKELETON. The tag list below is a placeholder until the collections
      are specified. Each collection gets one file in abstract/collections.md,
      and the tag that identifies it is decided there, not here. Adding a
      name to this list before that file exists is how the last content model
      grew by accretion — see abstract/09-lessons.md.
    ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── --}}
    {{#is "post"}}
      {{#post}}
        {{#has tag="#lesson, #docs-page"}}{{else}}{{> "navbar/navbar"}}{{/has}}
      {{/post}}
    {{else}}
      {{> "navbar/navbar"}}
    {{/is}}

    {{> "navbar/sheet"}}

    <main id="main" tabindex="-1">{{{body}}}</main>

    {{#is "post"}}
      {{#post}}
        {{#has tag="#lesson, #docs-page"}}{{else}}{{> "footer/footer"}}{{/has}}
      {{/post}}
    {{else}}
      {{> "footer/footer"}}
    {{/is}}

    {{!-- One bundle, built by gulp from NSDS's own script layer. All of it is
         progressive enhancement: the sheet, the theme controls, the outline,
         the reading line and the code copy button each degrade to something
         that still works. theme-init is deliberately NOT in it. --}}
    <script src="{{asset "built/main.min.js"}}" defer></script>

    {{!-- ── 10 · Analytics, last on purpose ───────────────────────────────
         Every service here is opt-in: nothing loads, and no socket opens,
         until its key is set in Admin → Design → Site-wide. A site with no
         keys ships no analytics code at all.

         END OF BODY, not the head. gtag is ~417 KB decompressed and PostHog
         ~267 KB; in the head they compete with the stylesheet and the fonts
         for the connection that decides LCP. Head placement buys only the
         counting of sub-second bounces, which is a rounding error next to a
         slower page for everyone.

         BEFORE {{ghost_foot}} so the consent gate is defined before any
         code-injection script an admin pastes there can look for it. --}}
    {{> "services/analytics"}}

    {{ghost_foot}}

</body>
</html>
